trils, as a petrel; tubinarial. See ''Tubinares.''

'''tube-plate''' (tūb′plāt), ''n.'' In steam-boilers, same as ''flue-plate.''

'''tube-plug''' (tūb′plug), ''n.'' In locomotive engines, a plug for driving into the end of tubes when burst by the steam.

'''tube-pouch''' (tūb′pouch), ''n.'' A pouch for holding priming-tubes.

'''tuber''' (tū′bėr), ''n.'' [< L. ''tuber'', a bump, swelling, tumor, knob on plants, truffle, etc.; perhaps < √ ''tu'' in ''tumere'', swell. Hence ult. prob. ''truffle.''] '''1.''' In ''bot.'', a subterranean body, usually of an oblong or rounded form, consisting morphologically of a stolon-like branch of a rhizome,
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|The rhizome of ''Krigia Dandelion'', showing the tubers, ''T'', at the end of the long stolons, ''S'', and one larger tuber from which the plant has been developed; ''C'', stem, underground; ''R'', roots.}}}}
much thickened, commonly at the end, and beset with “eyes,” which are properly modified axillary buds. {{smaller|Some of these buds normally sprout the second season, giving rise to a new plant, for the nourishment of which the tuber is richly stored with starch. Typical examples are the common potato and the Jerusalem artichoke (see ''Helianthus'', with cut); less familiar are the tubers of the dwarf dandelion (''Krigia Dandelion''), the American ground-nut (''Apios tuberosa''), and the ground-nut}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Tuber of Potato (''Solanum tuberosum'').}}}}
{{smaller|of Great Britain, ''Conopodium denudatum'' (''Bunium flexuosum''). Moniliform tubers occur, as in ''Equisetum fluviatile'' (see ''moniliform'') and ''Hydrocotyle Americana'' (see ''Hydrocotyle''). Strictly, the tuber is to be distinguished from the tubercle (see ''tubercle'' (''d'') (3)) and the tuberous root}}

